<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qarshi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qarshi is a city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>. In the 1320s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kebek-khan">Kebek Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Empire</a> attempted to make it a stable sedentary capital. Together with his currency reform, this represented the most serious attempt to transform the khanate into a territorial state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qarshi.jpg" alt="Qarshi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bobyrr · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monument_in_memory_and_honor_in_Qarshi.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Qarshi was originally the Sogdian city of Nakhshab, which may have been called Eucratideia under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom</a>. It later became the Islamic Turkic city of Nasaf and then the Chagatay city of Qarshi, pronounced Kharsh; the modern name means fort. It lies in a fertile oasis producing wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, and was a stop on the 11-day caravan route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>. The Chagataid khans Kebek and Qazan built palaces there, on the site of Chinggis Khaan's summer pasture, and in 1364 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> built a fortified palace with moats.</p><p>The town developed steadily from the 14th century and grew rapidly under the Sheyban dynasty in the 16th. In the 18th century it was the second largest town of the Bukhara Khanate and, with the decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahrisabz">Shahrisabz</a>, the seat of the crown prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>. It then had a double set of walls, ten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a> and four madrassahs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> annexed the Zarafshan Valley by 1868, and in 1873 the treaty making Bukhara a Russian protectorate was signed in Qarshi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Qarshi is the capital of Qashqadaryo Region in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, about 520 km south-south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>, and is known for natural gas and for woven flat carpets. The Odina Mosque, built in the 16th century on the site of an older Mongol palace that had also been a prison, houses the regional museum. Kok Gumbaz, the Blue Dome, is the city's Friday mosque and resembles other Timurid mosques, including those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahrisabz">Shahrisabz</a>. An old brick bridge crosses the Qashqadaryo River, and three madrassahs on one square were built between 1904 and 1915.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qarshi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The city of <b>Qarshi</b> in Uzbekistan takes its name from the palace that Kebek Khan built there, near Nasaf, in the early 14th century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 555–567, perhaps in 557, the Turks and Khosrow I allied against the Hephthalites and defeated them after an eight-day battle near <b>Qarshi</b>, the Battle of Gol-Zarriun.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
